 ### CUDA Isosurface Extraction with Overlapped Execution
-An optimization for CUDA-based isosurface extraction that overlaps data transfers and kernel executions to hide latency [S5]. It executes three overlapping pairs of operations: 1) calculation of vertices and transfer of triangles from the previous slice pair; 2) calculation of triangles and transfer of vertices; 3) compaction of triangles and transfer of the next slice [S5]. Constant memory is used for the lookup table and isovalue [S5]. Device initialization takes ~4 seconds, negligible for large datasets but significant for small ones (<100 MB) [S5]. For a 2048x2048x6936 dataset (23 GB), 16 GTX480 devices achieved speedups of 752.3x (isovalue 1000) and 1489.7x (isovalue 4000) [S5].
+An optimization for CUDA-based isosurface extraction that overlaps data transfers and kernel executions to hide latency [S2]. It executes three overlapping pairs of operations: 1) calculation of vertices and transfer of triangles from the previous slice pair; 2) calculation of triangles and transfer of vertices; 3) compaction of triangles and transfer of the next slice [S2]. Constant memory is used for the lookup table and isovalue [S2]. Device initialization takes ~4 seconds, negligible for large datasets but significant for small ones (<100 MB) [S2]. For a 2048x2048x6936 dataset (23 GB), 16 GTX480 devices achieved speedups of 752.3x (isovalue 1000) and 1489.7x (isovalue 4000) [S2].
 
 ### Finite State Machine for Interpolant Modelling
-A representation of isosurface evolution by sweeping from high to low isovalues, where states are cases and transitions occur when a vertex changes from white (lower than $h$) to black (higher than $h$) [S6]. The FSM is constructed by defining states as cases and adding transitions from each state to each other case with one extra black vertex [S6]. Each cube starts with case 0. For example, case 2 (two edge-adjacent black vertices) cannot sweep to case 7 (no edge-adjacent black vertices) because topology forbids losing edge adjacency [S6]. This is used to model watertight Marching Cubes cases and prove completeness of Nielson's cases [S6].
+A representation of isosurface evolution by sweeping from high to low isovalues, where states are cases and transitions occur when a vertex changes from white (lower than $h$) to black (higher than $h$) [S3]. The FSM is constructed by defining states as cases and adding transitions from each state to each other case with one extra black vertex [S3]. Each cube starts with case 0. For example, case 2 (two edge-adjacent black vertices) cannot sweep to case 7 (no edge-adjacent black vertices) because topology forbids losing edge adjacency [S3]. This is used to model watertight Marching Cubes cases and prove completeness of Nielson's cases [S3].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
 *   **Speed:** The improved MC algorithm (with 24 configurations and midpoint selection) is approximately 30% faster than the standard MC algorithm and 40% faster than the Masala algorithm [S4, abstract only, unofficial].
 *   **Parallelism:** Uses multi-thread parallel processing with 3 thread types for 24 configurations, synchronized via critical regions [S4, abstract only, unofficial].
-*   **Memory:** Uses constant memory for lookup tables and isovalue in GPU implementations [S5].
-*   **Accuracy:** Increases topological configurations to 24 to avoid voids [S4, abstract only, unofficial]. Resolves face-saddle ambiguities using the Asymptotic Decider [S3, unofficial].
-*   **Use Cases:** Medical image 3D reconstruction, where accuracy and speed are critical [S4, abstract only, unofficial]. Also used when data resolution increases such that triangle count exceeds pixel count, generating points instead of triangles [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Memory:** Uses constant memory for lookup tables and isovalue in GPU implementations [S2].
+*   **Accuracy:** Increases topological configurations to 24 to avoid voids [S4, abstract only, unofficial]. Resolves face-saddle ambiguities using the Asymptotic Decider [S3].
+*   **Use Cases:** Medical image 3D reconstruction, where accuracy and speed are critical [S4, abstract only, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Marching Cubes 2 was introduced by Nielson & Hamann in 1991 in the paper "The asymptotic decider: Resolving the ambiguity in marching cubes" [S3, unofficial]. It was developed to address the face-saddle ambiguities present in the original trilinear interpolant used in the standard Marching Cubes algorithm [S3, unofficial]. The variant with 24 configurations and midpoint selection was later proposed in a 2020 paper for improved 3D reconstruction speed and void avoidance [S4, abstract only, unofficial].
